WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.238
This is Chapter 6: PIM
for Azure resources.

4
00:00:09.238 --> 00:00:13.240
Standing Owner and Contributor are
what an attacker hopes to find,

5
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.314
so this chapter makes those roles
eligible, time-bound and approved,

6
00:00:17.314 --> 00:00:20.588
and holds the settings as
reviewed code at every scope.

7
00:00:20.588 --> 00:00:25.496
We'll start from Learn's walkthroughs
and Microsoft's own request examples,

8
00:00:25.496 --> 00:00:28.346
see what they leave to
you, then walk the plan,

9
00:00:28.346 --> 00:00:31.275
the dry run, the apply,
two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:31.275 --> 00:00:36.780
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:41.680
Why does this matter?

12
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.792
Because a standing Owner is
privilege that's always on.

13
00:00:44.792 --> 00:00:47.889
Nobody has to activate it,
approve it, or explain it,

14
00:00:47.889 --> 00:00:51.817
so an attacker who takes the
account doesn't have to wait either.

15
00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:54.910
The fix is the one you
already know from Entra roles:

16
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.781
make the role eligible,
time-bound and approved.

17
00:00:57.781 --> 00:01:01.555
What's different in Azure is that
every scope keeps its own rules.

18
00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:06.358
The chapter governs the three roles the
plan names, Owner, Contributor and User

19
00:01:06.358 --> 00:01:11.880
Access Administrator, with Microsoft's
ceiling of three subscription owners on top.

20
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.993
Next, which cloud.

21
00:01:16.993 --> 00:01:21.951
This estate's tenant is GCC on the
worldwide endpoints, with commercial Azure,

22
00:01:21.951 --> 00:01:26.755
so every call in this chapter goes to
the commercial Resource Manager host.

23
00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:29.957
GCC High and Department
of Defense subscriptions

24
00:01:29.957 --> 00:01:32.855
live in Azure Government,
which has its own host.

25
00:01:32.855 --> 00:01:36.863
The licensing PIM needs is
available in all of these clouds,

26
00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:41.199
but notice the honest gap: no page
the chapter read states the PIM

27
00:01:41.199 --> 00:01:45.780
Resource Manager API's availability
in Azure Government outright.

28
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.555
So the code makes you name
the cloud every time,

29
00:01:48.555 --> 00:01:51.690
and refuses a file written
for a different one.

30
00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.301
Here's the first thing to know:

31
00:01:57.301 --> 00:02:00.790
PIM for Azure resources isn't
in Microsoft Graph at all.

32
00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:03.658
Entra roles and groups
are managed through Graph,

33
00:02:03.658 --> 00:02:08.065
but Azure resources go through Resource
Manager, with no Graph permissions.

34
00:02:08.065 --> 00:02:11.982
Three resource types do the work:
one for eligible assignments,

35
00:02:11.982 --> 00:02:16.577
one for active assignments and activations,
and one for the role settings.

36
00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:21.403
The schedules they produce are the same
lists Chapter 1's inventory already reads.

37
00:02:21.403 --> 00:02:25.896
And there's a deadline: the old
beta endpoint stops returning data

38
00:02:25.896 --> 00:02:30.750
at the end of October 2026, so the
chapter uses only the current API.

39
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.188
Three facts shape the code.

40
00:02:36.188 --> 00:02:41.381
First, the version: the generally available
release covers every PIM resource type,

41
00:02:41.381 --> 00:02:44.166
and the code pins it, even
though one of Learn's

42
00:02:44.166 --> 00:02:46.725
how-to pages still shows an older preview.

43
00:02:46.725 --> 00:02:48.387
When Learn's pages disagree,

44
00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:52.650
the chapter follows the stricter one
and writes the disagreement down.

45
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.850
Second, settings don't inherit.

46
00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:59.050
A resource group under a governed
subscription keeps its defaults,

47
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.150
so the file has to list every scope.

48
00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.487
Third, only users and
groups can be eligible.

49
00:03:04.487 --> 00:03:09.050
A pipeline's service principal can't
activate, so it can't be converted.

50
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.512
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

51
00:03:14.512 --> 00:03:17.509
Learn has three walkthroughs, one per job:

52
00:03:17.509 --> 00:03:21.387
eligible access, active
access, and the role settings.

53
00:03:21.387 --> 00:03:24.927
Together they show how to
list, grant, remove, activate,

54
00:03:24.927 --> 00:03:30.075
and patch a role's rules, and which errors
come back when a request breaks a rule.

55
00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:32.988
Their source isn't public,
so they can't be pinned.

56
00:03:32.988 --> 00:03:37.380
But Microsoft's request and response
examples for the same calls are,

57
00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.926
so the repository pins six of them,
unmodified, with hashes a test checks.

58
00:03:41.926 --> 00:03:45.683
A stand-in Resource Manager then
replays them through the code,

59
00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:48.570
and nothing signs in
or leaves the machine.

60
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.358
Replaying the examples
turns up some real traps.

61
00:03:55.358 --> 00:03:58.688
The list examples show
one page for one person,

62
00:03:58.688 --> 00:04:01.669
but a busy subscription spans many pages.

63
00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:04.959
One example's role ID and
its friendly name disagree,

64
00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:08.707
so code that trusts the name
files a row under the wrong role.

65
00:04:08.707 --> 00:04:12.675
Another example says "assigned",
but it's really an activation,

66
00:04:12.675 --> 00:04:15.770
and read naively it looks
like standing access.

67
00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:20.458
The scope is written three different
ways, which quietly breaks any diff.

68
00:04:20.458 --> 00:04:23.192
And the sample eligibility lasts a year,

69
00:04:23.192 --> 00:04:26.671
with no justification,
longer than any tier allows.

70
00:04:26.671 --> 00:04:29.150
Each of these is pinned by a test.

71
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.425
The rest of the gaps aren't mistakes,
they're things the examples never try to do.

72
00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:40.426
They show one rule patched
at one subscription,

73
00:04:40.426 --> 00:04:43.888
but settings don't inherit,
so every scope needs its own.

74
00:04:43.888 --> 00:04:47.975
Nothing in them finds standing Owner,
which is the whole point of the project.

75
00:04:47.975 --> 00:04:52.587
Every write is a request you send by
hand, with nothing to review first.

76
00:04:52.587 --> 00:04:55.438
And every request goes
to the commercial host.

77
00:04:55.438 --> 00:04:59.614
So the verdict: use the walkthroughs
as the procedure and the examples

78
00:04:59.614 --> 00:05:03.650
as the contract for shapes, but
don't drive the estate from them.

79
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.338
Now the code.

80
00:05:08.338 --> 00:05:13.067
It's a second module that imports
Chapter 1's and changes nothing in it,

81
00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:16.801
so every read is declared,
paged to the end, and recorded.

82
00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:18.788
The call list has two tasks.

83
00:05:18.788 --> 00:05:20.538
Reading needs only Reader.

84
00:05:20.538 --> 00:05:24.302
Writing needs User Access
Administrator, which can manage role

85
00:05:24.302 --> 00:05:28.563
assignments but can't change the
resources themselves, the way Owner can.

86
00:05:28.563 --> 00:05:32.772
There's one more candidate, the
role-based access control administrator,

87
00:05:32.772 --> 00:05:34.737
which holds the same write action.

88
00:05:34.737 --> 00:05:38.882
But the PIM pages name only Owner
and User Access Administrator,

89
00:05:38.882 --> 00:05:43.630
so the code takes the stricter
reading until a lab proves otherwise.

90
00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.505
The heart of the configuration
is one reviewed file.

91
00:05:50.505 --> 00:05:55.257
It names the scopes, the roles, who's
eligible, and the settings for each tier,

92
00:05:55.257 --> 00:05:57.439
taken from Book 04's role settings:

93
00:05:57.439 --> 00:06:02.269
shorter activations and tighter
eligibility for the most sensitive tier,

94
00:06:02.269 --> 00:06:07.255
approval by a named group, and a
justification and ticket on every activation.

95
00:06:07.255 --> 00:06:10.005
Those values are the
program's design guidance.

96
00:06:10.005 --> 00:06:13.855
The loader also refuses what
Learn or the program rules out.

97
00:06:13.855 --> 00:06:16.454
The subtle one is authentication context:

98
00:06:16.454 --> 00:06:21.207
Learn says to create its Conditional
Access policy before you point PIM at it,

99
00:06:21.207 --> 00:06:24.330
so the file can't use it
until that's confirmed.

100
00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.353
The read is straightforward:
at each governed scope,

101
00:06:31.353 --> 00:06:34.093
both schedule lists and
one policy per role.

102
00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:38.431
A schedule counts as permanently
active only when it's assigned,

103
00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:41.379
never ends, isn't an
activation, and is direct.

104
00:06:41.379 --> 00:06:43.330
The plan is where order matters.

105
00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:48.093
Policies go first, because a request
that breaks the policy is refused.

106
00:06:48.093 --> 00:06:50.304
New eligible assignments go second.

107
00:06:50.304 --> 00:06:52.676
And standing access is removed last,

108
00:06:52.676 --> 00:06:55.917
so nobody loses a role
before they can activate it.

109
00:06:55.917 --> 00:06:59.960
Anything eligible that the file
doesn't mention is reported,

110
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.210
not removed: taking access away
is its own reviewed change.

111
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.635
The plan also reports what it found, and
just as important, what it won't touch.

112
00:07:12.635 --> 00:07:16.310
Standing privileged roles outside
owned exceptions are findings.

113
00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:21.097
A service principal is reported, never
converted, because it can't activate.

114
00:07:21.097 --> 00:07:23.986
Root-scope elevation is
flagged for removal.

115
00:07:23.986 --> 00:07:27.982
The identity running the plan
never plans away its own access;

116
00:07:27.982 --> 00:07:30.647
that's decided last, from another session.

117
00:07:30.647 --> 00:07:34.510
And a subscription that would end up
with more than three owners is flagged.

118
00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.588
Writing, meanwhile, is
hard to do by accident:

119
00:07:37.588 --> 00:07:42.205
it's a dry run unless you ask to
apply, and every run leaves evidence,

120
00:07:42.205 --> 00:07:46.060
including the state as read,
which is your back-out.

121
00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:50.872
Who is this for?

122
00:07:50.872 --> 00:07:53.169
The platform engineers who own the Azure

123
00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:56.073
management group hierarchy
write and keep the file.

124
00:07:56.073 --> 00:08:00.266
The identity engineers and the
information system security officers

125
00:08:00.266 --> 00:08:04.459
review every change before it's applied,
by reading the diff and the plan.

126
00:08:04.459 --> 00:08:08.500
The eligible holders are people
and groups, licensed for PIM,

127
00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:12.298
who'll now activate with a
reason, a ticket and an approval.

128
00:08:12.298 --> 00:08:17.552
And the automation itself runs with as
little as possible: Reader for the dry run,

129
00:08:17.552 --> 00:08:21.770
User Access Administrator held only
for a day or so for the apply,

130
00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:25.000
never Owner, and no
Graph permissions at all.

131
00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.800
Lab 1 runs on a laptop, with
no tenant and no network.

132
00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.263
First you replay Microsoft's own
examples, and the six tests pass.

133
00:08:37.263 --> 00:08:42.112
Read the second one: Microsoft's assignment
example is really an activation.

134
00:08:42.112 --> 00:08:45.849
Next, ask the code which
roles each task needs.

135
00:08:45.849 --> 00:08:47.638
Owner isn't needed for anything.

136
00:08:47.638 --> 00:08:51.475
Then plan against a small
fictional estate, as a dry run.

137
00:08:51.475 --> 00:08:56.668
It reads the schedules and policies, plans
eleven policy updates, five new eligible

138
00:08:56.668 --> 00:09:01.862
assignments and three removals, and writes
nineteen requests without sending one.

139
00:09:01.862 --> 00:09:03.610
Let's read what it planned.

140
00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.635
Now read the plan the
way a reviewer would.

141
00:09:09.635 --> 00:09:12.441
Three standing assignments
become eligible,

142
00:09:12.441 --> 00:09:16.797
each with a matching removal, and
two declared groups gain eligibility.

143
00:09:16.797 --> 00:09:21.648
Seventy policy rules change, but a policy
that's already right isn't touched.

144
00:09:21.648 --> 00:09:25.906
The findings tell the rest: the
automation's own access is left alone,

145
00:09:25.906 --> 00:09:30.772
a root-scope elevation is flagged, a
pipeline's service principal is reported,

146
00:09:30.772 --> 00:09:34.498
not converted, and one subscription
would have four owners.

147
00:09:34.498 --> 00:09:39.110
Then apply against the fake, and a
fresh read shows nothing left to change.

148
00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:44.500
Finally, watch the code refuse an undeclared
write and a scope it doesn't govern.

149
00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:52.812
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant and a
test subscription, never production.

150
00:09:52.812 --> 00:09:55.129
You give a reader app nothing but Reader,

151
00:09:55.129 --> 00:09:59.341
write a small desired state with one
hand-made standing Owner to convert,

152
00:09:59.341 --> 00:10:03.624
and plan live, comparing what the code
read with what the admin center shows.

153
00:10:03.624 --> 00:10:06.891
Then you grant the write role
for a single day, apply,

154
00:10:06.891 --> 00:10:10.313
and activate as the test user
to see the approval flow.

155
00:10:10.313 --> 00:10:15.001
There's also a small experiment to settle
an open question about the narrower role.

156
00:10:15.001 --> 00:10:17.668
And to back it out, wait
five minutes first:

157
00:10:17.668 --> 00:10:21.330
Learn says a new assignment
can't be removed sooner.

158
00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:29.378
Every danger here is a way a well-meant
change goes wrong in production,

159
00:10:29.378 --> 00:10:31.230
and each has a guard in the code.

160
00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:35.316
The worst is order: remove
standing Owner before the eligible

161
00:10:35.316 --> 00:10:39.242
role exists and someone loses
access in the middle of a task.

162
00:10:39.242 --> 00:10:44.405
Close behind is the automation removing
its own access halfway through a run.

163
00:10:44.405 --> 00:10:48.980
Others are quieter: a resource group
nobody listed keeps its defaults, an

164
00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:52.716
authentication context points
at a policy that isn't there,

165
00:10:52.716 --> 00:10:54.393
or a typo lands unreviewed.

166
00:10:54.393 --> 00:10:56.836
The pattern is the same as before:

167
00:10:56.836 --> 00:11:00.830
a dry run by default, and
rules that run every time.

168
00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:05.930
Here's what done looks like.

169
00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:08.954
On the estate side: every
governed scope is listed,

170
00:11:08.954 --> 00:11:13.822
and you've written down what isn't; each
role's settings match the tier everywhere,

171
00:11:13.822 --> 00:11:18.763
and a fresh read after the apply shows
nothing left; no standing privileged roles

172
00:11:18.763 --> 00:11:23.705
remain outside owned exceptions; and no
subscription has more than three owners.

173
00:11:23.705 --> 00:11:28.797
On the run side: the right roles, held
briefly, the right cloud, a reviewer who read

174
00:11:28.797 --> 00:11:33.967
the plan before the apply, and pinned
examples still matching what was reviewed.

175
00:11:33.967 --> 00:11:37.968
Next, Chapter 7 reviews who
holds these privileged roles.

176
00:11:37.968 --> 00:11:39.140
Thanks for watching.
